WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.162
Your driver works on a test board with ten frames, then the customer streams ten thousand and it falls over. Big O is how you predict that before shipping.

00:00:12.362 --> 00:00:13.291
Pause and think.

00:00:13.691 --> 00:00:19.578
If one loop is O of n and you nest a second loop inside it, what happens to the time when n doubles?

00:00:19.978 --> 00:00:27.292
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:28.492 --> 00:00:34.587
Welcome, Chandra. This lesson is about Big O, the language interviewers use to talk about speed.

00:00:35.037 --> 00:00:38.903
No math degree needed. We will use everyday pictures first.

00:00:40.253 --> 00:00:43.272
Imagine you are handing out name badges at a conference.

00:00:43.722 --> 00:00:51.977
If you just hand each guest the top badge from a pile, every guest takes the same tiny effort. That is constant time, written O of one.

00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:59.683
If you must search the whole pile for each guest's name, the effort grows with the number of guests. That is linear, O of n.

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:06.089
And if every guest must shake hands with every other guest, the work explodes. That is O of n squared.

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:09.389
Here is the picture that matters.

00:01:09.839 --> 00:01:13.996
The green line is constant: it does not care how big the input is.

00:01:14.446 --> 00:01:21.621
Log n grows very slowly. Doubling the input adds just one more step, like guessing a page in a book by halving.

00:01:22.071 --> 00:01:24.207
Linear grows in a straight line.

00:01:24.657 --> 00:01:27.002
n log n is what good sorting costs.

00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:33.048
And n squared shoots off the chart. With a hundred thousand items, that is ten billion steps.

00:01:34.398 --> 00:01:38.276
Big O describes the shape of the growth, not the exact seconds.

00:01:38.726 --> 00:01:43.788
We drop constants. Two n and n are both O of n, because the shape is the same.

00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:48.499
We keep only the biggest term. n squared plus n is just O of n squared.

00:01:48.949 --> 00:01:52.165
Space complexity uses the same language for memory.

00:01:52.615 --> 00:02:00.161
For your systems work, also remember that constants still matter in the real world: cache misses, allocations, and locks.

00:02:00.242 --> 00:02:11.388
New word: cache. A small fast store of recently used data so you don't fetch it from the slow place again. Picture it like this: Keeping the salt next to the stove instead of in the basement.

00:02:13.892 --> 00:02:15.924
Let us count the work in a real loop.

00:02:16.374 --> 00:02:22.283
Scanning an array once touches each cell once. Eight items, eight steps. That is O of n.

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:30.815
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:02:32.425 --> 00:02:39.438
A nested loop that compares every pair touches about n squared over two pairs. Eight items, twenty eight pairs.

00:02:39.888 --> 00:02:45.716
Binary search on sorted data cuts the range in half each step. Eight items, only three steps.

00:02:45.725 --> 00:02:54.618
New word: binary search. Find an item in sorted data by halving the range each step. Picture it like this: Guessing a number with "higher or lower".

00:02:57.168 --> 00:02:59.652
Here is a handy cheat sheet for interviews.

00:03:00.102 --> 00:03:04.015
If n is up to about ten thousand, n squared might pass.

00:03:04.465 --> 00:03:08.912
If n is a hundred thousand or a million, aim for n log n or n.

00:03:09.362 --> 00:03:14.888
If n is tiny, like twenty, even two to the n, exponential search, can work.

00:03:15.338 --> 00:03:21.038
Always say the complexity out loud after you code. It is one of the easiest points to earn.

00:03:22.388 --> 00:03:29.482
That is Big O. Next, open any problem in this course and you will see its brute force and optimal cost side by side.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:30.714
Back to the question.

00:03:31.114 --> 00:03:37.000
If one loop is O of n and you nest a second loop inside it, what happens to the time when n doubles?

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.033
It roughly quadruples: n squared grows four times when n doubles.

00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:44.103
Quick recap.

00:03:44.503 --> 00:03:47.429
Big O describes growth, not exact seconds.

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:50.418
Drop constants; keep the biggest term.

00:03:50.818 --> 00:03:53.860
Nested loops multiply; sequential loops add.
